// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 1 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P2): a runtime global-to-global text
// assignment. Before the fix, the only aliasing guard `cl_free_globals`
// had covered a global's own INIT expression, never a later store in a
// handler/function body -- `p2G2 = p2G1` copies `p2G1`'s handle into
// `p2G2` (Ch3 reference semantics), then `cl_free_globals` froze both:
// SIGSEGV. Now the whole-program globals escape pre-pass (lower.cla's
// `lowRunGlobalsEscapePrepass`, Task 8's window-var trick reused for
// globals) disqualifies `p2G1` (bare-ident RHS of an assignment is always a
// handoff) and `p2G2` (rule 4: reassigned from a non-fresh RHS) -- neither
// gets freed. Expected strict-mode leak count: 4 (see
// ../p2_global_assign.leaks): `p2G1`'s shared "one" handle (2 blocks,
// never freed since both names referencing it are disqualified) plus
// `p2G2`'s ORIGINAL "two" handle (2 blocks), orphaned the instant the
// assignment overwrites `p2G2`'s slot -- an inherent consequence of a
// plain handle-copy store (no old-value free on overwrite), not something
// this fix could or should change.

var p2G1: text = "one"
var p2G2: text = "two"

func runP2GlobalAssign() {
    p2G2 = p2G1
    alert("p2 done")
}
